  "generated_text": "1. The larger circle with center \\( B \\) has a radius of 6 inches.\n2. The smaller circle with center \\( A \\) has a radius of 3 inches.\n3. The shaded region is the area of the larger circle minus the area of the smaller circle.\n\nFirst, calculate the area of the larger circle:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of larger circle} = \\pi \\times 6^2 = 36\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate the area of the smaller circle:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of smaller circle} = \\pi \\times 3^2 = 9\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, subtract the area of the smaller circle from the area of the larger circle to find the area of the shaded region:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of shaded region} = 36\\pi - 9\\pi = 27\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27\\pi}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[27*pi, '27\\\\pi']",
    "gold_answer": "Since the small circle is tangent to the large circle at $C$ and point $B$ lies on the smaller circle and is the center of the larger circle, we know the radius of the bigger circle is twice the radius of the smaller circle, or six inches.\n\nTo find the shaded area, subtract the area of the smaller circle from the area of the larger circle.  $6^2\\pi - 3^2\\pi = 36\\pi - 9\\pi = \\boxed{27\\pi}$. \\[ - OR - \\] Consider the tangent line to circle $B$ at $C$, say line $l$.  Then $BC \\perp l$.  But since circle $A$ is tangent to circle $B$ at $C$, we also have that $AB \\perp l$.  Hence $A$ is on segment $BC$, and $BC$ is a diameter of circle $A$.  Thus by homothety circle $A$ covers $\\frac{1}{4}$ the area of circle $B$.  The shaded region is thus $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the area of circle $B$, and hence is 3 times the area of circle $A$, or simply $(\\pi \\cdot 3^2)\\cdot 3 = 27\\pi$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
